.env
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
.import_linter_cache/
.ruff_cache/
node_modules/

# Scratch file used to hold a DB password during local verification. Never commit.
.pwenc*
*.tmp

# Runtime logs. Rotated on disk, never in git - they contain user ids, job urls, and the shape of
# real traffic. `libs/obs` writes them here by default; LOG_DIR overrides.
logs/

# Local account credentials for the two migrated accounts. Plaintext, deliberately outside git.
# Regenerate rather than recover if lost - set_password.py overwrites.
accounts.local.txt

# Vite build output. Cloudflare Pages builds it; the repo carries the source.
web/dist/

# Every credential and certificate the platform needs, and no remote may ever see. One folder, so
# there is one place to look and one place to back up — API keys, tokens, certs, and the key below.
#
# **`SECRETS_KEK.env` is the one the nightly dump deliberately does not contain.** A restore without
# it yields a database of credentials nobody can decrypt, which `backup.py` says on every run. It
# lived in exactly one place, `deploy/.env` on the VPS, so the box dying took the key and every
# proven-restorable backup with it.
#
# What is here is a COPY of the live key, never a new one: `generate_key()` mints a fresh key, and
# swapping it would orphan every stored secret — both Telegram bots, both Claude credentials, the
# proxy. Verified against the live value by hash, so the value itself was never printed.
certificates/

# Trained model artifacts, one or more per account. Bind-mounted into the scorer and trainer so the
# backup and the pruning tools can see them (they could not while this was a named volume). Never in
# git: they are per-person classifiers, megabytes each, and rebuildable by a retrain.
#
# Anchored to the root: unanchored, it also matched `libs/db/models/`, so a new table's module was
# ignored without a word — committed nowhere, and an ImportError on the box.
/models/

# Archived postings — retention writes them here before deleting the rows (`libs.corpus.archive`).
# Never in git: it is real job text at whatever volume the corpus turns over, and on the box it is
# the ONLY remaining copy, so it belongs in the off-box backup rather than in a repository.
archive/

# Rendered CVs and cover letters from a local docgen run. Same two reasons as `scripts/coverlab/`
# below: each one is a real person's contact details and whole career, and each is regenerable from
# the profile that produced it. `3dbe1b5` committed one by accident.
out/

# The cover-letter bench: prompt variants, job fixtures, the judge, and one HTML letter per
# (variant x posting). Experimental and deliberately local.
#
# Three separate reasons, any one of which is enough:
#  - It is an experiment. Prompt variants under `variants/` are candidates that have not been
#    approved, and a candidate prompt sitting in the repository next to the real one is exactly the
#    "two copies, and the enforced one is not the visible one" failure CLAUDE.md #1 is about.
#  - Its fixtures are a real person. `profile.json` and `base_cv.html` are one candidate's contact
#    details and whole career; every generated letter repeats them.
#  - Its output is regenerable and large. Megabytes per run, and a new run replaces it.
#
# `libs/scoring/coverchecks.py` is the durable half and IS tracked: it is the measurement, and the
# numbers in any prompt discussion have to be reproducible from something in the repository.
scripts/coverlab/

# Real people's profile files, pulled from production for the CV bench. Two users' CVs, career
# histories and writing prompts — their own work, not the platform's, and never in git.
# `scripts/cvlab/run.py --candidate` reads them from here; re-pull with the query in that file.
scripts/cvlab/candidates/
scripts/cvlab/out/
